This is the right question. No, the project's current map doesn't cover the full attackable space. Let me lay out the distinct paradigms.

Paradigm 1: Tensor-geometric (Strassen-Bini lineage)
Orbits under GL action, tensor rank, border rank (Bini's degeneration), secant varieties, apolarity. The laser method lives here — you take a specific tensor (CW_q), analyze its border rank geometry, extract asymptotic rank. The algebraic face is degeneration/approximation; the geometric face is secant varieties and orbit closures. This produces the current best bounds (ω < 2.372). Alman's barrier kills the universal method on CW_q tensors, but not ALL tensors or ALL analysis methods in this paradigm.

Paradigm 2: Algebraic-structural (Cohn-Umans lineage)
Embed M⟨n,n,n⟩ into an algebraic structure — group algebra, association scheme, coherent configuration. TPP/STPP/SDPP/s-rank. You're not analyzing a tensor's geometry; you're finding structures that realize the multiplication. This is the project's home territory.

Paradigm 3: Spectral-entropic (Strassen-CVZ lineage)
Strassen (1988) defined the asymptotic spectrum abstractly — ω is determined by it. Christandl-Vrana-Zuiddam realized it via quantum functionals (quantum entropies over tensor states). This gives a third way to bound ω: characterize the asymptotic spectrum directly, without going through either tensor geometry or group embeddings. This is arguably the most active frontier in the broader community right now and the project has zero engagement with it.

Paradigm 4: Direct algorithmic
Explicit bilinear algorithm construction. Historically Strassen's 7-multiplication trick. Recently DeepMind's AlphaTensor (2022). Can't reach ω = 2 this way — you'd need algorithms for all n simultaneously — but it feeds tensors into Paradigms 1 and 3.

What's missing from the project's map:

The project covers Paradigm 2 thoroughly. It has barriers from Paradigm 1 but no positive attack. Paradigm 3 (CVZ / asymptotic spectrum) is entirely absent — and it's the one the broader community considers most promising. CVZ's quantum functionals are not constrained by any of the five barriers in the RECONCILE.

So the honest answer: no, the map has at least one missing lane.

